Adds `+24 B` - @marvinhagemeister

```jsx
let afterSetState;

class App extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { a: 0 };
    afterSetState = async () => {
      await new Promise(r => this.setState({ a: 1 }, r));
      this.setState({ a: 2 });
    };
  }
  render() {
    return <Tester a={this.state.a} />;
  }
}

class Tester extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { n: 0 };
  }
  static getDerivedStateFromProps(_, prevState) {
    return { n: prevState.n + 1 };
  }
  componentDidMount() {
    this.setState({ dummy: "hoge" }, afterSetState);
  }
  render() {
    return <div className="App">result: {String(this.state.n)}</div>;
  }
}
// 1st - mount 0 -> 1 (_nextState: null, state: { n: 1 })
// 2nd - setState 1 -> 2 (_nextState: { n: 2 }, state: { n: 2 })(_nextState===state)
// 3rd - updateProps 2 -> 3 (_nextState: { n: 2 }, state: { n: 3 }) <- incorrect!
// (_nextState should be { n: 3 } or null)
// 4th - updateProps 2 -> 3 (_nextState: { n: 2 }, state: { n: 3 }) <- :(
```

There are two ways to solve this:

- make `c._nextState` and `s` the same reference to reflect the changes
- assign null to `c._nextState` after applying the changes

In the former way, it is possible to avoid unnecessary copies in setState.

